To retrieve the username from the JWT token in ASP.NET C# WebApi, you can use the `JwtSecurityTokenHandler` class to decode the token and extract the username claim. Here's an example:

```csharp
using System;
using System.IdentityModel.Tokens.Jwt;
using System.Security.Claims;

public class JwtTokenHelper
{
    public static string GetUsernameFromJwtToken(string jwtToken)
    {
        var tokenHandler = new JwtSecurityTokenHandler();
        var key = Encoding.ASCII.Parse("your_secret_key");
        var token = tokenHandler.ReadJwtToken(jwtToken, new TokenValidationParameters
        {
            IssuerSigningKey = key,
            ValidateIssuer = false,
            ValidateAudience = false,
            ClockSkew = TimeSpan.Zero
        });

        return token.Claims.FirstOrDefault(c => c.Type == ClaimTypes.NameIdentifier)?.Value;
    }
}
```

Replace `"your_secret_key"` with the secret key used to sign the JWT token. You can call this method like this:

```csharp
var jwtToken = "your_jwt_token";
var username = JwtTokenHelper.GetUsernameFromJwtToken(jwtToken);
Console.WriteLine($"Username: {username}");
```

This will print the username claim from the JWT token.

To retrieve the username from the JWT token in ASP.NET Core Web API, you can use the `IHttpContextAccessor` to access the HTTP context and then extract the username from the token. Here's an example:

```csharp
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Http;
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Identity;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Logging;
using System.Security.Claims;
using System.Threading.Tasks;

public class UserService : IUserService
{
    private readonly IHttpContextAccessor _httpContextAccessor;
    private readonly UserManager<ApplicationUser> _userManager;
    private readonly ILogger<UserService> _logger;

    public UserService(
        IHttpContextAccessor httpContextAccessor,
        UserManager<ApplicationUser> userManager,
        ILogger<UserService> logger)
    {
        _httpContextAccessor = httpContextAccessor;
        _userManager = userManager;
        _logger = logger;
    }

    public async Task<string> GetUsernameAsync()
    {
        var context = _httpContextAccessor.HttpContext;
        if (context == null || context.User == null)
        {
            return null;
        }

        var claims = context.User.Claims;
        foreach (var claim in claims)
        {
            if (claim.Type == ClaimTypes.NameIdentifier)
            {
                return claim.Value;
            }
        }

        return null;
    }
}
```

Make sure to inject the `IHttpContextAccessor`, `UserManager<ApplicationUser>`, and `ILogger<UserService>` dependencies into your `UserService` constructor. You can then call the `GetUsernameAsync` method to retrieve the username from the JWT token.

