To calculate the distance between two points on the Earth's surface using the provided MATLAB code, you need to input the latitudes and longitudes of both points in radians. The code then calculates the distance between the two points considering the Earth's ellipsoidal shape.

For your example, San Francisco (37.7749° N, 122.4194° W) and London (51.5074° N, 0.1278° W), you can use the following code:

```matlab
% Input coordinates
lat1 = deg2rad(37.7749);
lon1 = deg2rad(-122.4194);
lat2 = deg2rad(51.5074);
lon2 = deg2rad(-0.1278);

% Calculate distance
[a, b] = ellipsoid_params('WGS84'); % Use WGS84 ellipsoid parameters
N1 = a / sqrt(1 - e2 * sin(lat1)^2);
N2 = a / sqrt(1 - e2 * sin(lat2)^2);
h1 = 0; % Assuming no height for San Francisco
h2 = 0; % Assuming no height for London
x1 = (N1 + h1) * cos(lat1) * cos(lon1);
y1 = (N1 + h1) * cos(lat1) * sin(lon1);
z1 = ((1 - e2) * N1 + h1) * sin(lat1);
x2 = (N2 + h2) * cos(lat2) * cos(lon2);
y2 = (N2 + h2) * cos(lat2) * sin(lon2);
z2 = ((1 - e2) * N2 + h2) * sin(lat2);
d = sqrt((x1 - x2)^2 + (y1 - y2)^2 + (z1 - z2)^2);

% Display result
fprintf('Distance between San Francisco and London: %.2f meters\n', d);
```

