```matlab
% Define a population of 100 rabbits with different fur colors
population = struct('fur_color',{'brown','white','gray'},'fitness',rand(100,1));

% Evaluate the fitness of each rabbit based on its fur color
fitness_function = @(rabbit) 1 - abs(rabbit.fur_color == 'brown'); % Higher fitness for brown rabbits
fitness_scores = fitness_function(population);

% Select the top 50% of rabbits with the highest fitness scores
selected_rabbits = population(sort(fitness_scores, 'descend'), :);

% Reproduce and replace the selected rabbits in the population
new_population = selected_rabbits;

% Repeat the process for multiple generations to simulate natural selection
num_generations = 10;
for i = 1:num_generations
    % Evaluate the fitness of the new population
    fitness_scores = fitness_function(new_population);

    % Select the top 50% of rabbits with the highest fitness scores
    selected_rabbits = new_population(sort(fitness_scores, 'descend'), :);

    % Reproduce and replace the selected rabbits in the population
    new_population = selected_rabbits;
end

% Display the final population
disp(new_population);
```

This code simulates a simple model of natural selection where rabbits with brown fur have a higher fitness than those with white or gray fur. Over multiple generations, the population becomes more dominated by rabbits with brown fur, which is a classic example of natural selection at work.

