To implement Auth0 SSO for multiple React apps, follow these steps:

1. Sign up for an Auth0 account and create a new application.
2. In the Auth0 dashboard, navigate to the "Applications" tab and click on your newly created application.
3. Click on the "Settings" tab and copy the "Domain" and "Client ID" values.
4. In each of your React apps, install the `@auth0/auth0-react` package using npm or yarn:
```bash
npm install @auth0/auth0-react
```
5. Create a new file called `auth0.js` in your React app's root directory and add the following code:
```javascript
import { Auth0Provider } from '@auth0/auth0-react';

const auth0 = new Auth0Provider({
  domain: 'YOUR_DOMAIN',
  clientId: 'YOUR_CLIENT_ID',
});

export default auth0;
```
Replace `YOUR_DOMAIN` and `YOUR_CLIENT_ID` with the values you copied from the Auth0 dashboard.
6. Wrap your app component with the `Auth0Provider`:
```javascript
import React from 'react';
import auth0 from './auth0';

function App() {
  return (
    <Auth0Provider {...auth0}>
      {/* Your app content goes here */}
    </Auth0Provider>
  );
}

export default App;
```
7. Now, you can use the `useAuth0` hook from the `@auth0/auth0-react` package to authenticate users in your React apps. For example:
```javascript
import { useAuth0 } from '@auth0/auth0-react';

function LoginButton() {
  const { loginWithRedirect } = useAuth0();

  return (
    <button onClick={() => loginWithRedirect()}>Login</button>
  );
}

export default LoginButton;
```
That's it! With these steps, you have implemented Auth0 SSO for multiple React apps. Users will be able to log in once and access all your applications seamlessly.

To set up Auth0 SSO for multiple React apps, follow these steps:

1. Sign up for an Auth0 account and create a new application.
2. In the Auth0 dashboard, navigate to the "Applications" tab and select your newly created application.
3. Click on the "Settings" tab and copy the "Domain" and "Client ID" values.
4. In each of your React apps, install the `@auth0/auth0-react` package by running the following command in your terminal:
```
npm install @auth0/auth0-react
```
5. In each app, import the necessary components and configure Auth0:
```javascript
import { Auth0Provider, useAuth0 } from '@auth0/auth0-react';

const auth0Config = {
  domain: 'YOUR_DOMAIN',
  clientId: 'YOUR_CLIENT_ID',
  redirectUri: 'YOUR_REDIRECT_URI',
};

function App() {
  return (
    <Auth0Provider {...auth0Config}>
      {/* Your app content goes here */}
    </Auth0Provider>
  );
}

export default App;
```
Replace `YOUR_DOMAIN`, `YOUR_CLIENT_ID`, and `YOUR_REDIRECT_URI` with the values you copied from the Auth0 dashboard.

Now, when users log in to any of your React apps, they will be authenticated with Auth0 and can access all the apps that share the same Auth0 configuration.

Here's a simple C function that converts an array of command-line arguments (`argv`) to a null-terminated string (`cstr`).

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

char *argv_to_cstr(int argc, char **argv) {
    int i;
    size_t len = 1; // Initialize with the first argument

    for (i = 1; i < argc; i++) {
        len += strlen(argv[i]) + 1; // Add the length of the current argument and a null terminator
    }

    char *cstr = malloc(len);
    if (cstr == NULL) {
        return NULL;
    }

    for (i = 0; i < argc; i++) {
        strcpy(&cstr[i * (strlen(argv[i]) + 1)], argv[i]);
    }

    return cstr;
}

int main(int argc, char **argv) {
    char *cstr = argv_to_cstr(argc, argv);
    if (cstr != NULL) {
        printf("Converted args: %s\n", cstr);
        free(cstr);
    } else {
        printf("Error allocating memory.\n");
    }

    return 0;
}
```

This function takes two arguments: `argc`, which is the number of command-line arguments, and `argv`, which is an array of pointers to those arguments. It allocates memory for a string that can hold all the arguments concatenated together, then copies each argument into the string and adds a null terminator at the end. Finally, it returns the converted string.

Here's a simple C function that converts an array of command-line arguments (`argv`) to a null-terminated string:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

char *convert_argv_to_string(int argc, char *argv[]) {
    int i;
    size_t len = 0;
    char *str = NULL;

    for (i = 0; i < argc; i++) {
        len += strlen(argv[i]) + 1; // Add one for the null terminator
    }

    str = malloc(len);
    if (!str) {
        return NULL;
    }

    i = 0;
    while (*argv[i] != '\0') {
        str[i] = *argv[i];
        i++;
    }
    str[i] = '\0';

    return str;
}

int main(int argc, char *argv[]) {
    char *str = convert_argv_to_string(argc, argv);
    if (str) {
        printf("Converted string: %s\n", str);
        free(str);
    } else {
        printf("Error allocating memory.\n");
    }

    return 0;
}
```

This function takes two arguments: `argc` (the number of command-line arguments) and `argv` (an array of pointers to the command-line arguments). It allocates memory for a null-terminated string, iterates over the `argv` array, and copies each argument to the string. Finally, it returns the converted string.

Here's a simple C function that converts an array of arguments (argv) into a null-terminated string:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

char *argv_to_string(int argc, char *argv[]) {
    int i;
    char *str = malloc(argc * sizeof(char));
    for (i = 0; i < argc; i++) {
        strcat(str, argv[i]);
        if (i < argc - 1) {
            strcat(str, " ");
        }
    }
    str[argc] = '\0';
    return str;
}

int main() {
    int argc = 3;
    char *argv[] = {"hello", "world", "!"};
    char *str = argv_to_string(argc, argv);
    printf("%s\n", str);
    free(str);
    return 0;
}
```

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

int main(int argc, char *argv[]) {
    char *cstring = (char *)malloc(sizeof(char) * (argc + 1));
    int i;

    for (i = 0; i < argc; i++) {
        strcat(cstring, argv[i]);
        if (i != argc - 1) {
            strcat(cstring, " ");
        }
    }

    printf("CString: %s\n", cstring);
    free(cstring);

