    \item Premise 1: All humans are mortal.
    \item Premise 2: Socrates is human.
    \item Conclusion: Therefore, Socrates is mortal.
\end{itemize}
This syllogism works by deductive reasoning, where the premises provide information that allows us to conclude the conclusion with certainty. The first premise states that all humans are mortal, which means that it is a universal statement about the nature of humanity. The second premise states that Socrates is human, which means that it is a specific statement about a particular individual. By combining these two premises, we can conclude that Socrates is mortal, which is a logical and necessary conclusion based on the information provided.

\begin{itemize}
    \item Premise 1: All humans are mortal.
    \item Premise 2: Socrates is a human.
    \item Conclusion: Therefore, Socrates is mortal.
\end{itemize}

This is a logical syllogism because the conclusion follows logically from the premises. The first premise states that all humans are mortal, which means they will eventually die. The second premise states that Socrates is a human, which means he is part of the same group as all other humans. Therefore, based on the first premise, it can be concluded that Socrates is also mortal and will eventually die.

\begin{enumerate}
    \item All humans are mortal. (Major premise)
    \item Socrates is human. (Minor premise)
    \item Therefore, Socrates is mortal. (Conclusion)
\end{enumerate}

This syllogism works by combining the major premise, which states that all humans are mortal, with the minor premise, which states that Socrates is human. The conclusion follows logically from these two statements, as it implies that since Socrates is human, he must also be mortal.

\begin{itemize}
    \item Premise 1: All humans are mortal.
    \item Premise 2: Socrates is a human.
    \item Conclusion: Therefore, Socrates is mortal.
\end{itemize}
This syllogism works by deductively reasoning from the general premise (all humans are mortal) to the specific conclusion (Socrates is mortal) based on the middle term (human).

