To configure your Chrome extension's boilerplate files to inject JavaScript on an existing webpage, you can follow these steps:

1. Create a new file named `inject.js` in your extension's folder. This file will contain the code for injecting the JavaScript into the target page.
2. In the `inject.js` file, add the following code to inject the JavaScript:
```javascript
chrome.tabs.query({active: true, currentWindow: true}, tabs => {
  const tabId = tabs[0].id;
  chrome.tabs.executeScript(tabId, {file: 'your_script.js'});
});
```
Replace `your_script.js` with the name of your JavaScript file that contains the code you want to inject into the target page.
3. In your manifest file (`manifest.json`), add the following code to include the `inject.js` file and enable the extension to run on all pages:
```json
{
  "manifest_version": 2,
  "name": "Your Extension Name",
  "version": "1.0",
  "description": "Your Extension Description",
  "permissions": ["activeTab"],
  "content_scripts": [
    {
      "matches": ["<all_urls>"],
      "js": ["inject.js"]
    }
  ]
}
```
This will allow your extension to run on all pages and inject the JavaScript from the `inject.js` file when the page is loaded.

To configure your Chrome extension's boilerplate files to inject JavaScript on an existing webpage, you can follow these steps:

1. Create a background script (e.g., `background.js`) that will handle the injection of JavaScript.
2. In your content script (e.g., `content.js`), add an event listener to detect when the page has loaded.
3. Once the page has loaded, use the `document.createElement` method to create a new script tag, set its `src` attribute to the path of your JavaScript file, and append it to the `head` element of the document.

