To configure the boilerplate files to inject JavaScript on an existing webpage, you can use the `chrome.runtime.executeScript` method in your Chrome extension's background script. This method allows you to execute a script in the context of the current webpage.

Here's a step-by-step guide:

1. Create a new file called `inject.js` in your Chrome extension's directory. This file will contain the JavaScript code you want to inject into the webpage.
2. In your `manifest.json` file, add the following code to the `"content_scripts"` section:
```json
{
  "matches": ["<all_urls>"],
  "js": ["inject.js"]
}
```
This will tell Chrome to inject the `inject.js` file into all webpages that the user visits.
3. In your `background.js` file, add the following code to the `chrome.runtime.onInstalled` event listener:
```javascript
chrome.runtime.onInstalled.addListener(function(details) {
  if (details.reason === 'install') {
    chrome.tabs.query({active: true, currentWindow: true}, function(tabs) {
      chrome.tabs.executeScript(tabs[0].id, {file: 'inject.js'});
    });
  }
});
```
This will ensure that the `inject.js` file is injected into the current webpage when the extension is installed.
4. Save your changes and test your extension by installing it in Chrome. The JavaScript code from the `inject.js` file should now be executed in the context of the current webpage.

Note that you can also use the `chrome.runtime.executeScript` method to inject JavaScript into specific webpages based on their URL or other criteria. You can find more information about this method in the Chrome Developer Docs: https://developer.chrome.com/docs/extensions/mv3/api/runtime/#executescript

