Here's a simple React utility that removes widows by adjusting the font size of an element if it exceeds the maximum width:

```javascript
import React, { useRef } from 'react';

const RemoveWidows = ({ children }) => {
  const textRef = useRef(null);

  const handleResize = () => {
    const textWidth = textRef.current.offsetWidth;
    const maxWidth = 800; // Adjust this value based on your desired maximum width

    if (textWidth > maxWidth) {
      const newFontSize = Math.floor(maxWidth / textWidth) * 100;
      textRef.current.style.fontSize = `${newFontSize}%`;
    }
  };

  return (
    <div ref={textRef} onResize={handleResize}>
      {children}
    </div>
  );
};

export default RemoveWidows;
```

To use this utility, simply wrap your content with the `RemoveWidows` component:

```javascript
import React from 'react';
import RemoveWidows from './RemoveWidows';

const App = () => {
  return (
    <RemoveWidows>
      <p>Lorem ipsum dolor sit amet, consectetur adipiscing elit.</p>
    </RemoveWidows>
  );
};

export default App;
```

Here's a simple React utility to remove widows:

```javascript
import React, { useState } from 'react';

const RemoveWidows = ({ text }) => {
  const [widows, setWidows] = useState([]);

  const handleTextChange = (event) => {
    const newText = event.target.value;
    const words = newText.split(' ');
    const lastLine = words.pop();

    if (lastLine && lastLine.length > 0) {
      setWidows((prevWidows) => [...prevWidows, lastLine]);
    } else {
      setWidows([]);
    }
  };

  return (
    <div>
      <textarea value={text} onChange={handleTextChange} />
      <ul>
        {widows.map((widow) => (
          <li key={widow}>{widow}</li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </div>
  );
};

export default RemoveWidows;
```

This component takes a `text` prop and displays it in a text area. As the user types, it splits the text into words and checks if the last word is longer than 0. If it is, it adds the last word to an array of widows. The widows are displayed in a list below the text area.

Here's a simple React utility to remove widows. This component takes in a paragraph and removes any lines that are too short, ensuring that no word is left alone on a line by itself.

```jsx
import React from 'react';

const RemoveWidows = ({ paragraph }) => {
  const lines = paragraph.split('\n');
  const newLines = lines.map((line) => {
    const words = line.split(' ');
    const lastWordIndex = words.length - 1;
    const lastWordLength = words[lastWordIndex].length;

    if (lastWordLength > 0 && words[lastWordIndex] !== '') {
      return line;
    } else {
      return '';
    }
  });

  return (
    <div>
      {newLines.join('\n')}
    </div>
  );
};

export default RemoveWidows;
```

To use this component, simply pass in the paragraph you want to remove widows from:

```jsx
<RemoveWidows paragraph="This is a sample paragraph with some words that are too short to be left alone on a line." />
```

This will output the modified paragraph with any lines that are too short removed:

```
This is a sample paragraph with some words that are too short to be left alone on a line.
```

