To update your application without replacing the existing EXE, you can follow these steps:

1. Create a new folder in the same directory as the existing EXE. Name it something like "update".
2. Extract the contents of the ZIP file into the "update" folder.
3. Modify the existing EXE to check for the presence of the "update" folder and if it exists, load the updated files from the "update" folder.
4. Restart the application to apply the updates.

Here's some sample code to demonstrate this approach:
```csharp
using System;
using System.IO;
using System.Reflection;

namespace MyApplication
{
    class Program
    {
        static void Main(string[] args)
        {
            // Check for the presence of the "update" folder
            string updateFolderPath = Path.Combine(Environment.CurrentDirectory, "update");
            if (!Directory.Exists(updateFolderPath))
            {
                Console.WriteLine("Update folder not found. No updates available.");
                return;
            }

            // Load the updated files from the "update" folder
            string updatedAssemblyPath = Path.Combine(updateFolderPath, "MyApplication.dll");
            Assembly updatedAssembly = Assembly.LoadFrom(updatedAssemblyPath);
            Type updatedType = updatedAssembly.GetType("MyApplication.Program");
            MethodInfo mainMethod = updatedType.GetMethod("Main", BindingFlags.Public | BindingFlags.Static);
            mainMethod.Invoke(null, new object[] { args });
        }
    }
}
```
In this example, the `Main` method checks for the presence of the "update" folder and if it exists, loads the updated `MyApplication.dll` file from the "update" folder. It then calls the `Main` method on the updated assembly to apply the updates.

Note that this approach assumes that the updated files are compatible with the current version of the application. You may need to modify the code to handle any compatibility issues that arise. Additionally, you may want to consider using a more robust updating mechanism, such as a software update framework, to manage updates for your application.

